  "query_preview": "..\n\nYesterday's high temperatures reached into the mid 60s across most \nareas (3 degrees from reaching the daily record high at Detroit). \nHigh temperatures should be just as warm today as a renewed surge of \nwarm advection arrives with a 500 mb ridge axis translating through \nduring the day. Strengthening low-level southwest flow as we head \ninto Saturday supports even warmer temps, as 925 mb temps reach \n14\u00b0C, suggestive of highs near 70 degrees. This would lead to daily \nrecord high temperatures being broken (see climate section). However,\nthe surface flow may remain backed enough out of the south- \nsoutheast to exert some cooler marine influence, particularly across \nWayne County, and thus NBM temps are only suggesting highs in the \nlower 60s for the Detroit Metro Area.\n\nA pattern change and colder temperatures are on track to arrive \nearly next week, as the 500 mb low over northern Alberta sinks south \ninto the Dakotas on Sunday. Models differ on the interaction and \nspeed of the upper-level trough over the Great Basin ejecting to the \neast. Sunday has a good shot of getting into the 60s once again if \nthe slower cold front timing of the Canadian/GFS solutions verifies.\nA healthy moisture axis for late November standards (PW values up to \n1 inch) arrives late in the afternoon into early evening. There \nlooks to be sufficient forcing and frontogenesis with the front to \nsupport a line of showers. Aggressive drying arrives by midnight, \nhowever, as an extremely dry Arctic air mass (PW values of 0.1 to \n0.25 inches) swings through the Great Lakes region Monday into \nTuesday. Nonetheless, a seasonably cold air mass with 850 mb temps \nlowering into the -10 to -12 C range looks to support isolated to \nscattered light snow showers and flurries, predominantly Monday \nevening and night, as soundings indicate saturation briefly within \nthe DGZ. An upper-level ridge quickly building in on Tuesday, per \nmodel consensus, will squash any lake-effect activity, even as 850 \nmb temps only slowly moderate.",
